{#
  Exports Trivy VulnerabilityReport contents to Loki, as the per-CVE half of
  the vulnerability dashboard.

  The reports already hold every CVE, but only severity counts reach Prometheus
  — trivy-operator's per-CVE metric is left off in trivy-operator-values.yml.j2
  because it creates a Prometheus series per CVE per container, tens of
  thousands of them, each holding a value that stays constant until the image
  is upgraded. Loki indexes only stream labels and leaves the line body
  compressed and unindexed, so the CVE identifier costs nothing to store there
  and is found by scanning at query time.

  Nothing pushes to Loki here. The CronJob prints to stdout, and Alloy — a
  DaemonSet already collecting every pod's logs through the Kubernetes API, see
  alloy-values.yml.j2 — ships it like any other container output. That is why
  the stream labels are namespace/pod/container: those are the labels Alloy's
  relabel rules attach, and this data inherits them rather than defining its
  own. Keeping the CVE out of a label is the whole point; putting it in one
  would rebuild the cardinality problem Loki was chosen to avoid.

  Output is logfmt, one line per vulnerability, which Grafana parses with
  LogQL's `| logfmt`. Fields are chosen to have no spaces in their values, so
  no quoting is needed: package names, versions, and CVE identifiers never
  contain one. Report titles and descriptions do, and are left out — the CVE
  identifier is the key to look the rest up.

  The scanned workload's namespace and container are emitted as
  workload_namespace and workload_container rather than the obvious names.
  Alloy attaches namespace, pod, container, and job as stream labels, and when
  `| logfmt` parses a field whose name already exists as a stream label, Loki
  keeps the label and renames the parsed field with an '_extracted' suffix.
  Queries would then have to know which of the two names to use, and the value
  under the plain name would be this CronJob's own namespace rather than the
  scanned workload's.

  Absent fields are emitted as 'none' rather than omitted. A vulnerability with
  no patch available has no fixedVersion, and an image referenced by digest has
  no tag; go-template renders a missing map key as '<no value>', which would
  put a space inside a logfmt value and split the field.

  ttlSecondsAfterFinished is deliberately long. Alloy discovers pods and tails
  their logs through the API, so a Job pod deleted promptly on completion can
  disappear before Alloy has read it. Ten minutes is far longer than the export
  takes and costs only a completed pod object sitting in the namespace.

  The schedule is independent of the operator's own scan cadence — this reads
  whatever the reports currently say, so a run between scans re-emits
  unchanged data. That is the cost of a snapshot export, and it is small:
  Loki stores the repeated lines compressed, and querying uses the most recent
  ones.

  Variables:
    trivy_namespace:        Namespace holding the Trivy Operator and this
                            CronJob (e.g. trivy-system).
    trivy_export_schedule:  Cron schedule for the export
                            (e.g. '0 */6 * * *').
    trivy_export_image:     Pinned kubectl image used to read the reports
                            (e.g. registry.k8s.io/kubectl:v1.35.8).
#}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: trivy-report-export
  namespace: '{{ trivy_namespace }}'
---
# Read-only, and only on the one report type this exports. The operator's other
# report kinds are not granted here even though the scanners producing them are
# disabled, so enabling one later does not silently widen what this can read.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: trivy-report-export
rules:
  - apiGroups:
      - aquasecurity.github.io
    resources:
      - vulnerabilityreports
    verbs:
      - get
      - list
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: trivy-report-export
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: trivy-report-export
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: trivy-report-export
    namespace: '{{ trivy_namespace }}'
---
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: trivy-report-export
  namespace: '{{ trivy_namespace }}'
spec:
  schedule: '{{ trivy_export_schedule }}'
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 1
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 600
      backoffLimit: 2
      template:
        spec:
          serviceAccountName: trivy-report-export
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          securityContext:
            runAsUser: 65534
            runAsNonRoot: true
            seccompProfile:
              type: RuntimeDefault
          containers:
            - name: export
              image: '{{ trivy_export_image }}'
              securityContext:
                allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
                privileged: false
                readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
                capabilities:
                  drop:
                    - ALL
              command:
                - kubectl
              # --cache-dir is required: kubectl writes its API discovery cache
              # under $HOME by default, which readOnlyRootFilesystem makes
              # unwritable. The emptyDir below is the only writable path.
              args:
                - get
                - vulnerabilityreports
                - --all-namespaces
                - --cache-dir=/tmp/kubectl-cache
                - -o
                # Folded scalar: each source line break becomes one space,
                # which is exactly the logfmt field separator. Record breaks
                # are emitted by an explicit newline action instead of literal
                # newlines, which the folding would otherwise eat.
                #
                # The closing tag is '+%}' rather than '%}'. Ansible renders
                # templates with trim_blocks enabled, which drops the newline
                # after a block tag — here that would pull the next key up onto
                # the end of this scalar and the manifest would not parse. The
                # '+' keeps that newline.
                - >-
                  {% raw %}go-template={{range .items}}{{$m := .metadata}}{{$r := .report}}{{range $r.vulnerabilities}}workload_namespace={{$m.namespace}}
                  workload={{index $m.labels "trivy-operator.resource.name"}}
                  workload_kind={{index $m.labels "trivy-operator.resource.kind"}}
                  workload_container={{index $m.labels "trivy-operator.container.name"}}
                  image={{$r.artifact.repository}}
                  tag={{if $r.artifact.tag}}{{$r.artifact.tag}}{{else}}none{{end}}
                  cve={{.vulnerabilityID}}
                  severity={{.severity}}
                  package={{.resource}}
                  installed={{.installedVersion}}
                  fixed={{if .fixedVersion}}{{.fixedVersion}}{{else}}none{{end}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}{{end}}{% endraw +%}
              resources:
                requests:
                  cpu: 50m
                  memory: 64Mi
                limits:
                  memory: 256Mi
              volumeMounts:
                - name: cache
                  mountPath: /tmp
          volumes:
            - name: cache
              emptyDir: {}
